from the darkness of the calais coast a  french special police team prepares to  break cover  they've been tracking men they believe  to be migrants and asylum seekers  planning to cross the channel  the group take off  the police pursuit is not about  arresting the men but halting the next  stage of their journey intercepting the  small boats they would have made the  crossing in  it was just about without motor only uh  commodity  this boat was almost certainly provided  by smugglers who will have been paid  thousands by those determined to make it  to uk shores  the following morning in sky news is on  board a plane scouring the calais coast  for signs of potential cross-channel  journeys  the pilot spots what he thinks a  migrants or asylum seekers on the beach  police are also alerted to a parked  vehicle with a boat attached and a  search begins for potential smugglers  they and the boats are the target of  this joint french uk operation stop them  the pilot says and people won't be put  in danger  [Music]  those who attempt the journey have  usually taken huge risks just to get to  french shores many escaping war others  poverty but the uk government is  determined to stem what it calls the  illegal trafficking people across the  channel and has handed millions in  recent months to the french to fund  operations on land in the air and at sea  we travel out with the maritime police  who regularly spot boats dangerously  overloaded with people they tell us the  smuggler's tactic is to try to send  multiple vessels out at once making it  difficult for officers to keep track on  them all and at sea there are limits on  what the police can actually do  many on the uk side have accused the  french of not doing enough to stop  migrants and asylum seekers crossing the  channel but once people are actually in  boats and out at sea the objective of  the police is simply to preserve life  even if that means trailing some would  say escorting boats towards uk waters  the french insist they have no legal  right to turn boats back they can only  offer help if needed a position which  has infuriated ministers in britain and  led many to question why the uk  government is funding these operations  at all  [Music]  despite the round-the-clock work on the  french side to thought the smugglers  record numbers of people have made it  across the channel in small boats so far  this year the threat from the uk side of  harsher penalties for those who enter  illegally seemingly little deterrent  michelle clifford sky news calais
 

